# profiler rules. scripts/install.sh copies this to ~/.irssi/scripts/profiler.rules
# when that file does not exist yet. Edit it, then /profiler reload.
#
#   <network> <channel[,channel...]> <min_trust>
#
# Warn when someone joins one of those channels with a trust score under
# min_trust (0 to 1). "*" in the network or channel field means "any". When
# several lines cover a channel, the highest min_trust wins.
#
# Trust runs from -1 (wearing somebody else's nick) to 1. Roughly: a nick seen
# the same way 3 times recently scores 0.25, 10 times 0.59, 20 times 0.76. A
# nick never seen before scores 0. Joins that contradict an established nick
# go negative and are always warned about, whatever min_trust says.
#
#   proxy <keyword or phrase>
#
# Adds a word to look for in the whois record of a joiner's address, on top of
# the built-in list (vpn, proxy, tor, hosting, digitalocean, ovh, ...). A match
# means "likely VPN, proxy or hosting provider" and gets its own warning.
#
#   notproxy <phrase>
#
# A whois record with this phrase is never a proxy, whatever keywords it has.
# Boost Mobile, for one, runs on Amazon's cloud, so its records say AMAZON.
# Lookups are cached for profiler_hostinfo_max_age, so an address already
# looked up keeps its old answer until then.
#
#   port <n[,n...]>
#   proxyport <n[,n...]>
#
# When profiler_probe is on, every joiner's address is knocked on to fingerprint
# the host. A port line replaces the default set of ports we knock on; with no
# port line the built-in set is used (22, 80, 443, 1080, 3128, 4145, 6667, 6697,
# 8080, 8118, 9050, 9051). A proxyport line adds to the ports that, found open,
# mean we connected straight through an open proxy and make the join suspect on
# its own (the built-in list is 1080, 3128, 4145, 8118, 9050, 9051). The probe
# result is cached with the whois for profiler_hostinfo_max_age. Banners are
# kept for review but don't affect the score.

efnet #CHAN 0.5

#libera #mychan,#otherchan 0.3
#* * 0.1

#proxy some hoster gmbh
notproxy boost mobile
